## Key Concepts
- Bit manipulation: isolating the least significant bit using two's complement arithmetic
- De Bruijn sequence mapping for constant-time position lookup
- Branchless, constant-time operations
- Proper edge-case handling for n == 0

## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to decompose a 128-bit operation into smaller parallel units and efficiently apply a well-known branchless counting technique.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount for 32-bit lanes
- 128-bit value decomposition into hi/lo components
- Branchless bit manipulation and final reduction

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount32(unsigned int x) {
    x = x - ((x>>1)&0x55555555);
    x = (x&0x33333333)+((x>>2)&0x33333333);
    x = (x+(x>>4))&0x0F0F0F0F;
    return (x*0x01010101)>>24;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount32(x.lo) + popcount32(x.lo >> 32) + 
           popcount32(x.hi) + popcount32(x.hi >> 32);
}
```

## Quick Tests
```c
assert(popcount128(0) == 0);
assert(popcount128(UINT128_MAX) == 128);
assert(popcount128(0xF0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0) == 32);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-twiddling without branches, a staple in low-level C/C++ interviews.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Constant-time bit tricks without loops
- Small lookup for nibble parity

## Code Example
```c
uint32 parity32(uint32 x){
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x &= 0x0F;
  return (0x6996 >> x) & 1;
}
```

